[{"category":"section","location":"#LogStatFunctions.jl","page":"Home","text":"Numerically stable logarithms of the mean, variance, and standard deviation of exponentials — a small companion to LogExpFunctions.jl.\n\nGiven an array A, these compute log-of-a-statistic-of-exp.(A) without ever forming exp.(A) (which would overflow for large entries):\n\nfunction computes\nlogmeanexp(A; dims=:) log.(mean(exp.(A); dims))\nlogvarexp(A; dims=:, corrected=true) log.(var(exp.(A); dims, corrected))\nlogstdexp(A; dims=:, corrected=true) log.(std(exp.(A); dims, corrected))\n\nusing LogStatFunctions\n\nA = 1000 .* randn(10^4)      # exp.(A) would overflow Float64\nlogmeanexp(A)                # finite and accurate\nlogstdexp(A; corrected=false)\n\nlogvarexp and logstdexp also accept logmean to reuse a precomputed logmeanexp(A; dims).\n\nEach function has an in-place variant (logmeanexp!, logvarexp!, logstdexp!) that writes the result into a preallocated output array, reducing over the singleton dimensions of out:\n\nA = randn(10, 5)\nout = zeros(1, 5)\nlogmeanexp!(out, A)          # same as logmeanexp(A; dims=1)\n\nAll three functions have ChainRules derivative rules for real arrays (loaded automatically when ChainRulesCore is in the environment), so they can be differentiated with ChainRules-based AD packages such as Zygote. Complex arrays are not covered by these rules.","title":"LogStatFunctions.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reference","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"function","location":"#LogStatFunctions.logmeanexp","page":"Home","text":"logmeanexp(A; dims=:)\n\nComputes log.(mean(exp.(A); dims)), in a numerically stable way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LogStatFunctions.logmeanexp"},{"category":"function","location":"#LogStatFunctions.logvarexp","page":"Home","text":"logvarexp(A; dims=:, corrected=true, logmean=logmeanexp(A; dims))\n\nComputes log.(var(exp.(A); dims)), in a numerically stable way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LogStatFunctions.logvarexp"},{"category":"function","location":"#LogStatFunctions.logstdexp","page":"Home","text":"logstdexp(A; dims=:, corrected=true, logmean=logmeanexp(A; dims))\n\nComputes log.(std(exp.(A); dims)), in a numerically stable way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LogStatFunctions.logstdexp"},{"category":"function","location":"#LogStatFunctions.logmeanexp!","page":"Home","text":"logmeanexp!(out, A)\n\nIn-place version of logmeanexp: computes log.(mean(exp.(A); dims)) over the singleton dimensions of out, and writes the result to out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LogStatFunctions.logmeanexp!"},{"category":"function","location":"#LogStatFunctions.logvarexp!","page":"Home","text":"logvarexp!(out, A; corrected=true, logmean=logmeanexp!(similar(out), A))\n\nIn-place version of logvarexp: computes log.(var(exp.(A); dims, corrected)) over the singleton dimensions of out, and writes the result to out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LogStatFunctions.logvarexp!"},{"category":"function","location":"#LogStatFunctions.logstdexp!","page":"Home","text":"logstdexp!(out, A; corrected=true, logmean=logmeanexp!(similar(out), A))\n\nIn-place version of logstdexp: computes log.(std(exp.(A); dims, corrected)) over the singleton dimensions of out, and writes the result to out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LogStatFunctions.logstdexp!"}]
